Even with restrictions on gambling adverts during sport, we need to shift funding and attention to practical protections that keep vulnerable viewers safe. I’d support a package of financial safeguards alongside stronger laws and licensing. Key measures would include:
- Deposit limits and loss limits on all gambling accounts, with default lifelong caps and easy, user-friendly controls to raise or lower them;
- Affordability checks and income verification for higher-stakes or high-frequency bets; prohibition on using credit for gambling;
- Clear, enforceable licensing conditions with independent audits, real-time monitoring, and robust penalties for breaches, plus mandatory funding for consumer protections and addiction services;
- A statutory framework requiring agencies and sports bodies to run public-awareness campaigns about responsible gambling, rather than relying on voluntary messages;
- On-screen, non-promotional prompts during broadcasts (time-outs, budget reminders, help-line info) to interrupt routine betting cues and steer viewers to support if needed.

I’ve seen families struggle with gambling debt when access is easy; deposit limits and enforced affordability checks would help families like mine avoid slipping into debt. A comprehensive, well-funded regulatory approach would be more effective than ads alone.

I think restricting gambling adverts during sport makes sense if it’s part of a broader package to protect vulnerable viewers. Alongside any restriction, we should invest in widely accessible responsible gambling awareness and practical tools. For example:
- public health campaigns about safer gambling across TV, radio, social media and in venues, funded by bookmakers but overseen by an independent body to keep messaging clear and non-sensational;
- better safety tools: easy-to-use self-exclusion, clear spending limits, and cooling-off periods with robust identity checks so they can’t be bypassed;
- education and outreach in communities: school and club programs, parent workshops and local talks in village halls to help people spot problem gambling early;
- replacement funding for grassroots sport and addiction support: a ring-fenced fund so clubs don’t lose money and vulnerable people still have access to help;
- visible in-venue messaging: information at stadiums and betting shops with helpline numbers and links to free online resources;
- digital platform controls: restrict targeted ads to vulnerable users and require responsible gambling messages by default.
From my experience, local clubs rely on sponsorship; any plan must balance protection with sustainable funding for sport.
